speechtext:
Hon. A. B. COPP (Secretary of State) moved that the House go into committee to consider the following proposed resolution:
That it is expedient to bring in a measure to make provision for the payment of claims for reparation for losses suffered by Canadian nationals during the late War. and to provide,-
(1) That the report of the commissioner appointed to enquire into and report upon the claims for reparation of Canadian nationals, upon approval by the Governor in Council, shall be deposited with the Secretary of State of Canada in his capacity as Custodian of Enemy Property;
i(2) That from time to time, as sums are received on account of reparation under the Agreement of London between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany, bringing into force what is commonly known as the Dawes plan, the amount thereof shall be paid by the Minister of Finance and Receiver General to the Secretary of State as Custodian, until such time as the aggregate of such payments is equal to the total amount of the claims for reparation as determined by the report of the said commissioner and approved by the Governor in Council;
(3)	That the Secretary of State shall, on receipt of such payments, from time to time distribute the same to persons found entitled to claims for reparation in the commissioner's report, by way of pro rata dividend;
(4)	That the Secretary of State shall annually lay before parliament, within fifteen days after the meeting thereof, a report of the receipts and expenditures under the proposed legislation.

We are really on the
subject now, because the only object in amending this section is to enable the election to be brought on suddenly. At the last election we had sixty days and there was no difficulty in appointing the returning officer;
and we can do that again. If the committee is of opinion that sixty days is necessary, now is the time to decide that question. If we leave the law as it is, sixty days must be given, or nearly sixty days at any rate, and I should think there would be no difficulty in that case in having the returning officers appointed.
